{% block doc %}

<section id="choosing">
  <h2>Which one should I choose?</h2>
  <p>Ask what event in GitHub genuinely marks "this is now live for users".</p>

  <div class="docs-rows">
    <div class="docs-row">
      <strong>Deployments (the default)</strong>
      <p>Choose this if your pipeline creates GitHub Deployments against an environment: web apps and services deployed by GitHub Actions, Vercel, Netlify, Railway, Heroku, Render, Argo CD and most other tooling.</p>
      <p>Distilled records a production ship when a <code>deployment_status</code> event arrives with state <code>success</code> for an environment recognised as production.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="docs-row">
      <strong>Releases</strong>
      <p>Choose this if shipping means publishing a version: libraries, SDKs, CLI tools, mobile and desktop apps, or anything consumed by tag rather than deployed by you.</p>
      <p>Distilled records a production ship when a release is <strong>published</strong>. Drafts and pre-releases are ignored, and no environment is needed.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>A typical workspace holds both: services set to deployments, packages set to releases. That's why the setting is per repository rather than per workspace.</p>
</section>

<section id="changing">
  <h2>Changing the setting</h2>
  <p>Profile menu → <strong>Settings → Deployment Tracking</strong>. Each repository has a two-option control; the change saves immediately. Owners only.</p>
  <p>Both sources write to the same underlying history, so switching does not reset or split your metrics. Deployments recorded before the switch and releases recorded after sit on one continuous timeline. What changes is which future events count.</p>
</section>

<section id="production-environments">
  <h2>How production environments are detected</h2>
  <p>For repositories tracking deployments, Distilled reads your repository's GitHub Environments when the repository is connected and marks an environment as production if its name contains <code>prod</code> or <code>live</code>, case-insensitively, anywhere in the name.</p>
  <p>That's deliberately generous. <code>production</code>, <code>prod-eu</code>, <code>web:live</code> and <code>acme / production</code> all qualify, and so does <code>preprod</code>. An over-counted environment shows up in your deployment numbers and can be corrected. A missed one shows up nowhere and drags your metrics down.</p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p><strong>Environments are discovered when a repository is connected.</strong> If you create a production environment on an already-connected repository, re-run <strong>Connect GitHub</strong> from Settings → Repositories so Distilled picks it up. Until then its deployments are skipped.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="no-deployments">
  <h2>If you don't use GitHub Deployments</h2>
  <p>You have two options.</p>
  <p><strong>Switch that repository to release tracking</strong>, if publishing a release is genuinely how it ships.</p>
  <p><strong>Or emit deployment events from your pipeline.</strong> Any CI system can create them with one API call at the end of a successful production deploy: create a deployment for the deployed commit with <code>environment: production</code>, then post a deployment status of <code>success</code> against it. That's all Distilled needs, and it's a standard GitHub API any pipeline can call.</p>
</section>

<section id="what-counts">
  <h2>What counts, precisely</h2>
  <div class="docs-table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Event</th><th>Counted?</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Successful deployment to a production environment</td><td>Yes, on repositories tracking deployments</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Failed or in-progress deployment</td><td>No, ignored until it resolves to success</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Successful deployment to staging or preview</td><td>No, unless the environment name contains <code>prod</code> or <code>live</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Published release</td><td>Yes, on repositories tracking releases</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Draft or pre-release</td><td>No</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Re-delivered duplicate of an event</td><td>Counted once; events are de-duplicated on GitHub's own id</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>If a repository tracks deployments but has no production environment, the delivery metrics that depend on shipping say <strong>"Requires a connected production environment"</strong> rather than showing a misleading zero. See <a href="/docs/data-quality">Data quality</a>.</p>
</section>

